From b1d115f8d3070a81f3e231acb86cc446656581fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 08:52:52 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 25/25] Browser: a 3D Canvas menu, per site

The camera hack was a preference for every page
(webgl.stereo-camera.enabled, off). The canvas context menu now has a
3D Canvas menu beside the video's 3D Video menu: Off or On, and four
depths (the eyes' separation: 1, 2, 3 or 4 percent of the view's
width), for the page's site. The choices are permissions
(stereo-camera: allow or deny; stereo-camera-depth: the separation in
tenths of a percent), which the content process reads for the canvas's
document at each present; without them the preferences decide. A change
takes effect with the canvas's next renderer, as the presented frames
change size. The depth reaches the WebGL host with each present
(SwapChainOptions::stereoDepth). Cameras are looked for wherever the
session can show stereo, whatever the site's choice, so that a site
turned on draws in stereo from the next frame even when its page
uploaded its camera once.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code
